I was trying to do a broad stroke so we wouldn't need to do any form of deep analysis.  Obviously, current situation has some unique aspects.  We are still normalizing from QE era suppression of long rates.  There has been excessive fiscal expansion.  The changing investor base for duration has been shifting to private investors that pay taxes from price insensitive official buyers that didn't.  That requires higher rates to clear.  

But every era has its own unique story.  Duration below 5% is mispriced for an investor that has to pay taxes when Fed targets 2% inflation.  We are normalizing to more rational levels but nothing scary here IMO

